DTSTART:20260929T223000Z
DTEND:20260930T000000Z
LOCATION:The Republic Building\, Columbus
GEO:39.199929;-85.921713
SUMMARY:John Sanders | J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lecture
DESCRIPTION:In this lecture\,"Seeds of Regionalism—The Clauss Legacy: E
 arly Modernism in the South\," John Lynch  Sanders\, FAIA\, explore
 s an early chapter in the architectural legacy of Alfred and Jane West Cl
 auss\, creators of America's first modern deed-restricted subdivision loc
 ated in Knoxville\, Tennessee's "Little Switzerland". Delivered from a fi
 rst-hand perspective\, Sanders details a 13-year immersive journey of con
 serving and restoring these historically neglected structures. The presen
 tation goes beyond traditional archival history to examine the performati
 ve\, physical processes of preservation. By analyzing the tactical interv
 entions required to uncover the original designs\, the lecture demonstrat
 es how the Clausses successfully synthesized international modernism with
  regional Appalachian topography and vernacular materials.Sanders is a 
 founding partner of Sanders Pace Architects\, established in 2002 to 
 focus on design opportunities within Knoxville's urban core and its surro
 unding neighborhoods. His passion for design is reflected in the firm's m
 ost challenging projects\, which prioritize the thoughtful rehabilitation
  of neglected buildings and the elevation of overlooked project typologie
 s. Beyond his practice\, Sanders is deeply committed to advancing the p
 rofession through teaching\, mentorship\, and civic engagement.An active 
 member of the American Institute of Architects (AIA)\, Sanders has serv
 ed as President of the AIA East Tennessee chapter and on the AIA Tennesse
 e State Board. Throughout his board tenures\, he was instrumental in crea
 ting a signature licensee program that formally recognizes and celebrates
  the milestones of emerging professionals at local and state levels.Sande
 rs is a graduate of the University of Tennessee\, where he has also share
 d his expertise as an Adjunct Lecturer. In recognition of his contributio
 ns to the discipline\, he was elevated to the prestigious AIA College of 
 Fellows in 2018 and in 2024 was awarded the AIA East Tennessee Gold Medal
 \, the chapter's highest honor\, along with his business partner Brandon 
 Pace\, FAIA.The J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lecture and Exhibiti
 on Series brings prominent working artists and architects to The Republic
  Building\, 333 Second Street in Columbus\, Indiana.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span data-contrast="none">In this lectur
 e\,</span><span data-contrast="none"><span class="TextRun SCXW24407167 BC
 X8" lang="EN-US" xml:lang="EN-US" data-contrast="none"><span class="Norma
 lTextRun SCXW24407167 BCX8">"<span class="NormalTextRun SCXW266699746 BCX
 8">S</span><span class="NormalTextRun CommentStart CommentHighlightPipeRe
 st CommentHighlightRest SCXW266699746 BCX8">eeds of Regionalism—The Cla
 uss Legacy: Early Modernism in the South\,"</span></span></span> John L
 ynch  Sanders\, FAIA\, </span><span data-contrast="none">explores an 
 early chapter in the architectural legacy of Alfred and Jane West Clauss\
 , creators of America's first modern deed-restricted subdivision located 
 in Knoxville\, Tennessee's "Little Switzerland". Delivered from a first-h
 and perspective\, Sanders details a 13-year immersive journey of conservi
 ng and restoring these historically neglected structures. The presentatio
 n goes beyond traditional archival history to examine the performative\, 
 physical processes of preservation. By analyzing the tactical interventio
 ns required to uncover the original designs\, the lecture demonstrates ho
 w the Clausses successfully synthesized international modernism with regi
 onal Appalachian topography and vernacular materials.</span><span data-cc
 p-props="{&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559685&quot\;:0\,&quot\;33
 5559739&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:240}"></span></p><p><span data-
 contrast="none">Sanders is a founding partner of Sanders Pace Archi
 tects\, established in 2002 to focus on design opportunities within Knoxv
 ille's urban core and its surrounding neighborhoods. His passion for desi
 gn is reflected in the firm's most challenging projects\, which prioritiz
 e the thoughtful rehabilitation of neglected buildings and the elevation 
 of overlooked project typologies. Beyond his practice\, Sanders is deep
 ly committed to advancing the profession through teaching\, mentorship\, 
 and civic engagement.</span><span data-ccp-props="{&quot\;134233117&quot\
 ;:false\,&quot\;134233118&quot\;:false\,&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\
 ;335551550&quot\;:1\,&quot\;335551620&quot\;:1\,&quot\;335559685&quot\;:0
 \,&quot\;335559737&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559738&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559739&
 quot\;:160\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p><p><span data-contra
 st="none">An active member of the American Institute of Architects (AIA)\
 , Sanders has served as President of the AIA East Tennessee chapter and
  on the AIA Tennessee State Board. Throughout his board tenures\, he was 
 instrumental in creating a signature licensee program that formally recog
 nizes and celebrates the milestones of emerging professionals at local an
 d state levels.</span><span data-ccp-props="{&quot\;134233117&quot\;:fals
 e\,&quot\;134233118&quot\;:false\,&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;33555
 1550&quot\;:1\,&quot\;335551620&quot\;:1\,&quot\;335559685&quot\;:0\,&quo
 t\;335559737&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559738&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559739&quot\;
 :160\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast="no
 ne">Sanders is a graduate of the University of Tennessee\, where he has a
 lso shared his expertise as an Adjunct Lecturer. In recognition of his co
 ntributions to the discipline\, he was elevated to the prestigious AIA Co
 llege of Fellows in 2018 and in 2024 was awarded the AIA East Tennessee G
 old Medal\, the chapter's highest honor\, along with his business partner
  Brandon Pace\, FAIA.</span><span data-ccp-props="{}"></span></p><p class
 ="p1"><i>The J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lecture and Exhibition 
 Series brings prominent working artists and architects to The Republic Bu
 ilding\, 333 Second Street in Columbus\, Indiana.</i></p>
