SUMMARY:Igor Siddiqui | Contingent Communities lecture
DESCRIPTION:Siddiqui's work—manifested as objects\, images\, texts\, an
 d events—links design experimentation to public engagement. He is inter
 ested in the role that interiors play in architectural experiments while 
 advocating for experimentation in interiors as a distinct field of knowle
 dge.Siddiqui's bookOblique Experiments: Claude Parent&#8217\;s Architectu
 ral Installations (1969–1975)is one of the first English-language monog
 raphs on French avant-garde architect Claude Parent and the first compreh
 ensive study of his experimental interior installations from the 1970s. H
 e recently editedInterior Experiments\, a special triple-issue ofInterior
 s: Design/Architecture/Culture\, where he has served as Editor-in-Chief\,
  Co-Editor\, and Associate Editor since 2016.His creative work has been e
 xhibited at venues including the Tallinn Architecture Biennale\, Contempo
 rary Austin\, SITE Santa Fe\, and Fusebox Festival\, and featured inDwell
 \,Interior Design\,Artforum\, andTexas Architect. In 2022\, Siddiqui rece
 ived the IIDA Educator of the Year Award. He previously taught at the Uni
 versity of Pennsylvania\, California College of the Arts\, and Parsons. S
 iddiqui is a registered architect in Texas.Learn more at:https://www.igor
 siddiqui.com@isssstudio on InstagramThe Eskenazi School's Comprehensive D
 esign and Interior Design programs bring renowned designers to campus for
  public lectures and studio visits as part of the Design Speaker Series.
