SUMMARY:Abelardo Morell: The Universe Next Door
DESCRIPTION:5:30-6:30 PM\, Artist talk and Q&amp\;A\,&#160\;Martin Common
 s\, second floor6:30-7:00 PM\, Enjoy Morell's photographs in The Photogra
 phic Reflex\, Featured Exhibition\, first floorAbelardo Morell's photogra
 phs reach through art history to one of the earliest predecessors of the 
 medium\, the camera obscura. In conjunction with the featured exhibitionT
 he Photographic Reflex: The Lee Marks and John C. DePrez Jr. Collection (
 https://artmuseum.indiana.edu/exhibitions/2026-08-29-photographic-reflex-
 marks-deprez.html)\, the photographer will speak about his artistic pract
 ice and delve into his remarkable career. He has continually returned to 
 the fundamental mechanics of light and vision\, using them to transform o
 rdinary rooms and landscapes into sites of quiet revelation.To make his m
 ost celebrated body of work\, Morell darkens an entire room and leaves a 
 single small opening in a window. Light passes through that aperture and 
 projects an inverted image of the outside world onto the interior walls. 
 City streets\, trees\, and skylines appear suspended across beds\, ceilin
 gs\, and wallpaper. Morell then photographs this projection\, joining int
 erior and exterior in a single frame. The process follows the basic laws 
 of optics\, yet the result feels magical and imaginative\, as the world o
 utside settles inside the room. This sustained investigation earned him a
  Guggenheim Fellowship in 1993 and remains central to his practice.Artist
  Bio:Abelardo Morell was born in Havana\, Cuba in 1948. He immigrated to 
 the United States with his parents in 1962.  Morell received his underg
 raduate degree from Bowdoin College and his MFA from The Yale University 
 School of Art. He was professor of Photography at the Massachusetts Colle
 ge of Art and Design in Boston from 1983 to 2010. His work is held in lea
 ding public collections worldwide and a major retrospective\, The Unive
 rse Next Door\, opened at the Art Institute of Chicago in 2013 and travel
 ed to the Getty Museum in Los Angeles and the High Museum of Art in Atlan
 ta.https://www.abelardomorell.net/
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span data-contrast="auto"><span class="N
 ormalTextRun SCXW15750192 BCX4"><strong>5:30-6:30 PM\,</strong> Artist ta
 lk and Q&amp\;A\,</span><span class="NormalTextRun SCXW15750192 BCX4">&#1
 60\;Martin Commons</span><span class="NormalTextRun SCXW15750192 BCX4">\,
  second floor</span></span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto"><span class=
 "NormalTextRun SCXW15750192 BCX4"><strong>6:30-7:00 PM\,</strong> Enjoy M
 orell's photographs in <em>The Photographic Reflex</em>\, Featured Exhibi
 tion\, first floor</span></span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto">Abelard
 o Morell's photographs reach through art history to one of the earliest p
 redecessors of the medium\, the camera obscura. In conjunction with the f
 eatured exhibition</span><i><span data-contrast="auto"><a href="https://a
 rtmuseum.indiana.edu/exhibitions/2026-08-29-photographic-reflex-marks-dep
 rez.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Photographic Reflex: The Lee
  Marks and John C. DePrez Jr. Collection</a></span></i><span data-contras
 t="auto">\, the photographer will speak about his artistic practice and d
 elve into his remarkable career. He has continually returned to the funda
 mental mechanics of light and vision\, using them to transform ordinary r
 ooms and landscapes into sites of quiet revelation.</span><span data-ccp-
 props="{}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto"></span><span data-ccp
 -props="{}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto">To make his most cel
 ebrated body of work\, Morell darkens an entire room and leaves a single 
 small opening in a window. Light passes through that aperture and project
 s an inverted image of the outside world onto the interior walls. City st
 reets\, trees\, and skylines appear suspended across beds\, ceilings\, an
 d wallpaper. Morell then photographs this projection\, joining interior a
 nd exterior in a single frame. The process follows the basic laws of opti
 cs\, yet the result feels magical and imaginative\, as the world outside 
 settles inside the room. This sustained investigation earned him a Guggen
 heim Fellowship in 1993 and remains central to his practice.</span><span 
 data-ccp-props="{}"></span></p><p><strong>Artist Bio:</strong></p><p><spa
 n data-contrast="auto">Abelardo Morell was born in Havana\, Cuba in 1948.
  He immigrated to the United States with his parents in 1962.  Morell r
 eceived his undergraduate degree from Bowdoin College and his MFA from Th
 e Yale University School of Art. He was professor of Photography at the M
 assachusetts College of Art and Design in Boston from 1983 to 2010. His w
 ork is held in leading public collections worldwide and a major retrospec
 tive\, </span><i><span data-contrast="auto">The Universe Next Door</spa
 n></i><span data-contrast="auto">\, opened at the Art Institute of Chicag
 o in 2013 and traveled to the Getty Museum in Los Angeles and the High Mu
 seum of Art in Atlanta.</span><span data-ccp-props="{}"></span></p><p><a 
 href="https://www.abelardomorell.net/"><span data-contrast="none">https:/
 /www.abelardomorell.net/</span></a><span data-contrast="auto"></span><spa
 n data-ccp-props="{&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:278
 }"></span></p>
